hi this is Brandon roar with some  thoughts on data scientist archetypes in  other words what kind of data scientists  do you want to be when you first come to  the field of data science even if you've  been in it for a while you can be easily  overwhelmed by the number of tools and  the vast range of skills required blog  posts about what it takes to become a  data scientist devolving to laundry list  of skills that you don't have yet and  tools you haven't learned yet this is  discouraging and it's counterproductive  my goal here is to give you a map for  navigating the sprawling terrain of data  science is to help you prioritize what  you want to learn and what you want to  do so that you don't feel lost these  ideas are not mine alone they're based  on a series of interviews with the data  scientists listed here who are from  several different companies as well as  contributors from academic data science  programs the underlying ideas are  captured in a public document offered as  a resource to academic data science  programs from industry data scientists  an attempt at a virtual industry  advisory committee any insights you get  from this material that I'm presenting  here I credit to this group but any  errors or inaccuracies are probably due  to me alone  overall this is a compilation of a lot  of people's ideas who have spent time  thinking deeply about it  the real reason data science feels so  big is because it's no longer a single  field there are actually three separate  pillars there's data analysis which is  taking raw information and turning it  into knowledge that can be acted on or  that can drive a decision there's data  modeling using the data that we have to  estimate the data that we wish we had  and then there's data engineering which  is taking these analysis and modeling  activities and making everything work  faster more robustly and on larger  quantities of data so these three areas  are all distinct they all require  different skills and different sets of  tools if you feel overwhelmed when you  approach data science it's because  you're actually approaching three  different fields at the same time now  let's dive a little deeper into each one  of these there are several aspects of  data analysis the process of taking data  and turning it into into information  that we can use to make a decision  there is domain knowledge this is the  ability to translate a business need or  a practical concern to a question it  involves making trade-offs between how  accurate the answer should be and how  much time and money you want to spend  finding it  domain knowledge is also absolutely  necessary for interpreting the data for  understanding not just the numbers and  labels presented but what they reflect  in the real world and what we can  realistically assume about their  accuracy another important part of  translating information into actions is  research this can be as straightforward  as performing the right Google search  and reading a few documents often it  involves instrumenting processes that  are already in place like logging code  or adding sensors to a production line  or conduct conducting surveys for some  applications research involves careful  design of experiments that requires  using sophisticated statistical tools to  plan what information will be gathered  how it will be gathered and how it will  be analyzed after the fact finally an  important part of data analysis is  interpretation given a large collection  of data how can we summarize it  aggregated visualize it and use  statistical summaries to go from a sea  of bits to a nugget of knowledge in  particular I want to call out the art of  visualization turning numbers into a  picture that is easily interpreted and  conveys useful information is an art as  much as a science it involves an  intuitive sense of human visual  perception and understanding  so the pillar of data modeling also has  several major subcategories this is  often referred to as machine learning  but it's no more or less than the  creating of a simplified description of  your data that you can use to make  estimates for data that you haven't  measured either something that you  didn't have a sensor for or something  that's going to happen in the future  the three main subclasses of machine  learning are supervised unsupervised and  custom model development supervised  learning uses labelled examples to  discover patterns these can be  categorical labels for example did this  customer revisit our website within the  month after making a purchase yes or no  examples can also be labeled with  numbers for example how many views did a  video receive using a large collection  of labeled examples accompanied either  by their category or value supervised  learning algorithms can distill out the  underlying patterns if the labourers  serve categorical this is called  classification and if the labels are  numerical this is called regression also  sometimes the goal is not to make a  specific prediction about future  examples but just to determine whether  they're in line with what's been seen  previously this is called anomaly  detection a second major area of machine  learning is called unsupervised learning  indicating that the data doesn't come  with labels we don't know the outcome or  the right answer but in unsupervised  learning the goal is to discover  patterns in how the data is distributed  in clustering groups of data points that  are nearer to each other than to the  rest of the data set are identified  these are the natural groupings in the  data for example this can be done to  find shoppers that buy similar items at  the grocery store in dimensionality  reduction groups of variables that tend  to behave similarly or in a coordinated  fashion are identified as well such as  the set of genes that work together to  produce a desired trait  in dimensionality reduction it's not the  data points that are being clustered but  the variables it's not the rows of the  table but the columns in apply data  modeling it's very common to have to do  custom algorithm development both  supervised and unsupervised learning  methods are general they begin by  assuming very little about the source of  the data what it means and what domain  it resides in general algorithms get  away with this by sheer statistical  power they're at their best when you  have a large collection of data however  in practical applications is often the  case that we don't have nearly as much  data as we would like and we don't have  enough to rely on naive mature machine  learning techniques when this happens we  get to do custom algorithm development  we build in the information that we know  or feel comfortable assuming about the  domain for instance one important  modeling problem in the agriculture  industry is to predict the yield for a  field of corn that's the total weight of  the harvested grain this is an  unfathomably complex system with soil  and texture chemistry genetics  temperature and moisture all interacting  and it takes a year to collect a single  data point there's no feasible way to  learn a complete crop model using lie  Eve naive machine learning methods  however geologists chemists biologists  meteorologists and geneticists have all  investigated and described the pieces of  this system and so by assembling what is  known and making some reasonable  assumptions we can create a tractable  tractable crop model moving on to data  engineering this is the third pillar of  data science it has a few major areas of  its own data management is the exercise  of storing moving and handling data it's  trivial for data sets of a few thousand  or even a few million data points but  when individual data points become very  complex like an individual's genome or  the number of data points gets into the  billions or trillions data no longer  fits inside Ram on a laptop and you have  to use an entirely new set of tools  another big task the data engineers have  is taking code that works well in  prototype and making it ready for  operation in the wider world  it is one thing to build a model in  Jupiter notebook but to be able to use  that to process and to filter every  incoming customer request the codes need  to the code needs to be made compatible  with the rest of the company's code base  and it needs to be able to ingest data  and publish results in the appropriate  formats any glitches or failures need to  be handled gracefully and the system  needs to be made secure against  mischievous or malicious users the gap  between a smoothly running prototype and  production code is a big one and part of  data engineering is the process of  closing it in both data management and  production software engineering plays an  important role that's the act of  building things out of code that not  only do what you want as fast as you  want but also can be shared and  maintained across your current team  adapted to future changes and scaled up  or down as needs evolve this is a craft  and it comes with a large collection of  tools and specialized skills there's  also a surprise pillar number for data  mechanics this is the dirty work that  everyone needs to do but nobody likes to  talk about it includes things like data  formatting making sure your types are  consistent getting information you want  out of strings making sure the strings  in use are valid handling errors  gracefully it also includes value  interpretation handling dates and times  in an interpretive interpretable and  consistent way responding correctly to  missing values making sure units of  measurement or  assistant undocumented and it includes  the fundamentals of data handling  querying slicing and joining this lets  you pull the subset of data that you  want for an operation from the great  lake of what you have available now we  have our four pillars in place data  analysis data modeling data engineering  and data mechanics what does this mean  for you as a data scientist these areas  are all very different I propose that a  data scientist is someone who has at the  very least exposure to all these skill  areas that means that you're not  necessarily an expert or even good at  them but you know what they are and you  know the right questions to ask and the  right Google searches to run a beginner  data scientist might have just a little  bit of experience in each of these  pillars with this as a base we can walk  through some data scientist archetypes  it's my hope that these will be helpful  to you in planning out your training and  career trajectory one path forward to  becoming a data science scientist is to  become a generalist to develop a solid  mixture of proficiency and analysis  modeling engineering and mechanics a  nice balance across the board gives you  a broad view of problems of varying  scopes it may mean that you'll need to  collaborate with specialists for some  projects but it sets you up very well to  be a technical leader or a data  executive just a word of caution there  may be a tendency to neglect data  mechanics or to assume that someone more  junior will take care of it the person  who does this is a data scientist diva  this is an anti-pattern this is someone  you do not want to be and you do not  want to hire mechanics can get gritty  and some days it'll occupy the majora  your time but you never outgrow the need  to do it  embrace mechanics tasks learn the skills  you need to do them adequately and get  on with it now there are three  specialist archetypes as well  emphasizing each of the three main  pillars if you choose to emphasize a -  analysis you can become a detective a  master in discovering the right data and  using it to draw conclusions a detective  is familiar with modeling and  engineering methods and uses them in a  lightweight way but analysis is their  focus another data scientist archetype  is the Oracle someone who has mastered  the skills of modeling and the tools of  machine learning they will have had  exposure to analysis and engineering but  their focus will be on modeling either  general machine learning models or  custom domain specific models finally  you can choose to emphasize data  engineering and become a maker this is a  person who helps everything work fast  and reliably their work will still  require some analysis and some modeling  and definitely some mechanics but  they'll be focused on engineering tasks  makers are the ones who transform good  ideas into concrete machinery finally  there is the unicorn this person is a  master in every pillar this is the data  science ideal except of course that it  doesn't exist becoming a master of one  pillar takes a long time becoming a  master in all three is essentially  stacking three careers on top of each  other it's not impossible to do but it's  a long road and the path down it passes  through one of the other archetypes as a  waypoint the archetypes then that are  the most useful for career planning are  the generalist detective Oracle and the  maker  another way to represent these is in a  3d plot with axes for analysis modeling  and engineering you can see the  detective Oracle and maker each lying  close to an axis the generalist is a  balance of the three sitting in the  middle of the space I want to be  completely clear that I am not implying  any hierarchy or value judgment between  the archetypes they're each invaluable  and they serve different functions let  your preferences and interests guide you  as you choose between them there's no  commonly accepted taxonomy for data  science specializations when looking for  a job  I recommend ignoring the job titles  there's no reliable way to translate  between job title and archetypal I also  recommend not worrying too much about  the specific tools listed languages  programs you ought to know instead look  at the skills implied read between the  lines to discover which archetype they  want just the posting sound more like a  maker a generalist a detective based on  this you can decide whether this  particular posting is a good match for  you also when posting a job ask for  specific skills don't ask for all of  them and don't ask for a unicorn you  won't find one what I hope you take away  from this more than anything is that you  don't have to be an expert at everything  to be a great and successful data  scientist it's enough to choose one  direction and dive in good luck if you  haven't already picked your path I hope  this helps you plan it and I wish you  lots of success along the way

